-- title:
--   Unique polynomial normal forms for commuting operators in a cyclic algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $A$ be a commutative $K$-algebra, and let $x\in A$. Suppose evaluation $q\mapsto q(x)$ maps $K[t]$ onto $A$. Let $m\in K[t]$ be monic, and assume that its principal ideal is exactly the evaluation kernel:
--   $$
--   q(x)=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad m\mid q.
--   $$
--   For $b\in A$, let $L_b(a)=ba$. Every $K$-linear endomorphism $T:A\to A$ satisfies
--   $$
--   \bigl(\forall a\in A,\ T(xa)=xT(a)\bigr)
--   \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
--   \exists! q\in K[t],\quad \deg q<\deg m\ \land\ T=L_{q(x)}.
--   $$
--   This gives unique polynomial coordinates for operators in the commutant of $L_x$. Degrees take values in $\{-\infty\}\cup\mathbb N$, with $\deg0=-\infty$, so zero representatives and degree-zero monic kernel generators are included. No nontriviality of $A$ or finite-dimensionality hypothesis is imposed.
-- source:
--   Derived supporting algebra lemma for the Senthil Kumar mission, combining proved platform theorems polynomial_cyclic_commutant (a572f240-2d39-4863-9c86-3b965e40549b) and polynomial_monic_evaluation_normal_form (76abba6e-731b-4203-96db-b9ebee7f957e). If evaluation at x surjects onto a commutative K-algebra and its kernel is generated by a specified monic polynomial m, commuting linear operators have unique polynomial representatives of degree below degree(m). A polynomial representing the multiplier is reduced modulo m; injectivity of the regular representation gives uniqueness. K is any field. Trivial target rings and degree-zero monic m are allowed; no finite-dimensionality premise is imposed. This is inferred supporting algebra, not a separately quoted theorem of the source article. Mathlib revision 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474: Algebra.lmul_injective in Algebra/Algebra/Bilinear.lean lines 154-155; polynomial remainder facts in Algebra/Polynomial/Div.lean. Primary documentation: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Algebra/Bilinear.html and https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Polynomial/Div.html . No new definitions.

import Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Div
import Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.AlgebraMap
import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Bilinear

theorem WeierstrassEllipticZeta.polynomial_cyclic_commutant_normal_form
    (K A : Type*) [Field K] [CommRing A] [Algebra K A]
    (x : A) (m : Polynomial K) (hm : m.Monic)
    (hker : ∀ q : Polynomial K, q.eval₂ (algebraMap K A) x = 0 ↔ m ∣ q)
    (hx : Function.Surjective (fun q : Polynomial K => q.eval₂ (algebraMap K A) x))
    (T : A →ₗ[K] A) :
    (∀ a : A, T (x * a) = x * T a) ↔
      ∃! q : Polynomial K, q.degree < m.degree ∧
        T = Algebra.lmul K A (q.eval₂ (algebraMap K A) x) := by sorry
